# Changelog

All notable changes to the ZGeo-PG QGIS plugin are documented in this file.
Entries are written in English, newest first, and match the version in `metadata.txt`.

## [0.29.0] - 2026-11-18

### Added

- The reference data statistics has a fourth row, "Everything from the left
  panel is present in the right", whose value is only "Yes" or "No".
- The verdict is repeated in the tab status bar, written to the "Statistics"
  sheet of the Excel export and to the HTML report, and its right-click menu
  entry lists the values that are missing on the right.

## [0.28.0] - 2026-11-18

### Added

- `html_export.write_html_report()` builds a report page: a sticky link panel on
  top, then "Selected sources", "Statistics" and one table per category.
- `table_export.export_report_with_dialog()` saves the same report as Excel
  (a "Statistics" sheet plus one sheet per category) or HTML.

### Changed

- The reference data export lists what was chosen in both panels (connection,
  table, field) before the statistics and the values.
- The Excel file now always contains the "Statistics" sheet with the category
  counts, so the workbook matches the HTML page.

### Fixed

- `write_html_sections()` and the report produced a nested `<tr><tr>` in table
  headers because the header row was wrapped twice.

## [0.27.0] - 2026-11-18

### Changed

- The state dot of both reference data sides sits left of the connection list,
  in its own narrow grid column, so it reads as part of the combo box.
- An empty table list is explained instead of showing
  "Connected, but no tables are available" for every case:
  "Connected, but the database has no tables: <name>" when the database is
  empty, and "Connected, but no privilege to read tables: <name> (tables in the
  database: N)" when tables exist but the role cannot read them.
- When nothing is readable, `list_tables()` returns the full list anyway, so
  the table combo is not empty and picking a table reports the exact privilege
  error.

## [0.26.0] - 2026-11-18

### Changed

- `reload_connections()` walks the saved connections instead of taking the
  first one: it keeps the preferred (last used) connection when it opens on a
  database with tables, otherwise it takes the first working entry, and only
  falls back to a connection without readable tables when nothing else opens.

## [0.25.0] - 2026-11-18

### Added

- A state dot next to the "Connection" list of both reference data sides: green
  when the connection is open, red when it is not, with the error text as a
  tooltip.
- Picking an entry in the "Connection" list connects to it right away, and
  picking the same entry again reconnects (the `activated` signal is handled
  along with `currentIndexChanged`).

### Fixed

- An open connection without readable tables used to leave an empty table list
  with no explanation. It is reported as "Connected, but no tables are
  available" and the dot stays green, so an empty list is no longer confused
  with a failed connection.
- `list_tables()` returned an empty list when its query failed; it raises with
  the readable error and the side turns red with the reason in the status bar.
- The sides preselect the last used connection instead of the first entry of
  the list, and the automatic probe on list refresh no longer opens a message
  box for a connection that cannot be opened.
- A saved connection that lives only in the SQLite file is re-synced before it
  is declared missing, and an unknown name now says "Connection not found:
  <name> / Save it first" instead of failing silently.

## [0.24.0] - 2026-11-18

### Fixed

- Tables and views the user has no SELECT privilege on are no longer listed in
  the reference data combos or in the spatial table list, so a table like
  `nsi_field_name` can no longer be picked and fail on read. Both queries now
  filter with `pg_catalog.has_table_privilege(c.oid, 'SELECT')`.
- A privilege error is reported as "No access to the table: schema.table"
  instead of the raw "ОШИБКА: нет доступа к таблице ... (42501) QPSQL:
  Невозможно создать запрос" text, which the status bar also prefixed with
  another "Ошибка:".
- `list_text_columns()` raised nothing before and silently returned an empty
  column list; it now raises with that readable message.

### Changed

- The PostGIS bookkeeping relations (`geometry_columns`,
  `geography_columns`, `spatial_ref_sys`, `raster_columns`,
  `raster_overviews`) are hidden from the reference data table list.

## [0.23.0] - 2026-11-18

### Fixed

- Switching to a second connection on the "Reference data quality" tab closed
  the previous QtSql handle while it was still referenced, so Qt reported
  "connection is still in use" and every query of that side stopped working.
  `close_connection()` and `NsiSide._close_connection()` now drop the
  references before `removeDatabase()`.
- The connection lists of both sides show the connection the panel is using
  even when it was never saved, and they refresh on connect, disconnect, save
  and delete, so the second side can always be activated.
- A connection that cannot be opened reports the error in the status bar and
  in a message box and leaves the side usable instead of failing later with an
  empty table list.

### Changed

- `NsiSide` keeps its QtSql tag in `self._tag`, verifies `load_connection()`
  instead of silently ignoring a missing record, and shows
  "Connected: <name> (N)" after a successful switch.
- The frame of the tab surfaces the status of both sides through
  `show_status()` and `current_connection()` on the panel.

## [0.22.0] - 2026-11-18

### Added

- "Reference data quality" tab: the panel is split in two, each side picks a
  connection, a table and a text field and shows the sorted list of unique
  values below.
- "Compare" between the panels fills the statistics table with "Matched",
  "Present in the left panel but not in the right" and "Present in the right
  panel but not in the left".
- The statistics table has a right-click menu (and a double click) that opens
  the values of the chosen category in a list on the right.
- Every list and the comparison result can be exported: Excel writes each
  category to its own sheet, HTML writes every category as its own table on one
  page.

### Changed

- `nsi.py` holds the data access: table and text column listing, the sorted
  unique value query with a row limit, and `compare_values()` returning a
  `NsiComparison`.
- `html_export.write_html_sections()` and
  `table_export.export_sections_with_dialog()` write several titled sections in
  one file.
- The Nsi tab keeps its own connections per side, refreshes them when a
  connection is saved, restored or deleted, and closes them on plugin unload.

### Fixed

- `ZGeoPgPanel.__init__` had lost the index task attributes while the quality
  tabs were extracted, so unload and the spatial index actions raised
  AttributeError; the duplicated `_on_refresh_indexes_for_all` is gone too.

## [0.21.0] - 2026-11-18

### Added

- "Attribute data quality" tab with ten checks: NULL values, empty strings,
  leading and trailing spaces, double spaces, duplicate records, numbers stored
  as text, control characters, abnormal text length, outliers in numeric
  columns and dates out of the reasonable range.
- `quality.py` declares them as `AttributeCheck` records. A check names the
  column kind it needs (text, numeric, date or all), and the SQL is built from
  the columns reported by `fetch_attribute_columns()`, so one check covers
  every suitable column of a table.
- `AttributeTask` runs the selected checks over all loaded tables in a
  background thread on its own connection, with the same progress, abort and
  export behaviour as the spatial tab.

### Changed

- Both quality tabs are now `QualityTabPage` instances created by
  `_add_quality_page()`, so the check list, descriptions, results table,
  translations and the Excel/HTML export exist once. The panel gained
  `begin_progress()`, `progress_update()`, `set_progress_text()`,
  `set_progress_visible()` and `end_progress()` for the shared status bar.
- Only one quality run can be active at a time; starting a second one shows
  "Wait until the running checks finish".

### Fixed

- The attribute column query ignored primary keys, so an identity column was
  counted as a NULL-filled attribute; primary key columns are skipped now.
- Per-column checks wrapped their subqueries without a column alias and failed
  with a SQL error.

## [0.20.0] - 2026-11-18

### Added

- "Export" button on the quality tab: the check results are written to Excel
  or HTML with a totals row that sums every numeric check column.

### Changed

- Check titles, summaries, full descriptions and fix hints are part of the
  translation dictionary, so language switching also covers the description
  panel, the tooltips and the results table headers.
- Results are kept as raw values in `_quality_results` and rendered through
  `_render_quality_cell()` / `_render_quality_summary()`, so switching the
  language redraws "Not applicable", "Error" and the summary line without
  re-running the checks. The open description is re-rendered as well.

### Fixed

- The Russian description of the coordinate range check did not match its
  translation key and stayed untranslated.

## [0.19.0] - 2026-11-18

### Added

- "Geospatial data quality" tab with ten checks: invalid geometry, empty
  geometry, duplicate geometries, self-intersections, mixed geometry types,
  wrong SRID, coordinates out of range, degenerate features, unclosed rings and
  a missing spatial index.
- Every check has a checkbox and an "i" button that opens its full description
  and the way to fix the problem in a panel on the right.
- `quality.py` holds the checks as declarative `QualityCheck` records with one
  SQL template each, and `QualityTask` runs the selected checks over every
  loaded table in a background thread on its own database connection.
- The "Run checks" button executes the selected checks, "Abort" stops the run
  between queries; the current check name is shown above the status bar and its
  progress in the status bar.
- Results are shown as a table × check grid, problem cells are highlighted and
  the summary line counts the problems and the failed queries.

### Changed

- Tab titles are resolved through the stored page widgets, so the new fourth
  tab keeps its name after switching the interface language.

## [0.18.0] - 2026-11-18

### Changed

- Export writes every size as a human-readable string ("80 KB", "1.5 GB"):
  `export_rows_with_dialog()` accepts a `size_columns` argument and converts
  those cells from bytes with `format_size()`; the tables and statistics
  exports pass their size columns, so Excel and HTML now show the same values
  as the interface.
- HTML cells that start with a digit are right-aligned, so the converted sizes
  keep the numeric column alignment.

### Fixed

- `scripts/build_zip.ps1` closes QGIS Desktop before replacing the plugin
  directory (QGIS locks its Python modules) and starts it again afterwards.
  The new `-NoRestart` switch keeps QGIS closed.

## [0.17.0] - 2026-11-18

### Added

- "Indexes size" column: the size of all indexes except the spatial one.
- "Full size" column: data size + other indexes + spatial index.

### Changed

- `catalog.table_sizes()` returns `pg_table_size` and `pg_indexes_size`; the
  spatial index size is subtracted from the latter, so "Size" now means table
  data only and nothing is counted twice.
- The totals row, the information dialog, the statistics tab and both export
  formats use the new columns.

## [0.16.0] - 2026-11-18

### Added

- "Refresh all spatial indexes" button to the right of "Create all spatial
  indexes" on the "Tables" tab; it rebuilds the spatial index of every loaded
  table that has one.

## [0.15.0] - 2026-11-18

### Added

- The "Export" button opens a menu with "Export to Excel" and "Export to HTML";
  the statistics export offers the same choice.
- `html_export.write_html_table()` writes a self-contained HTML page with
  styled table, right-aligned numbers and a highlighted totals row.

### Fixed

- The exported table totals row now sums every numeric column (estimated rows,
  exact rows, table size and spatial index size) instead of shifting values
  into the wrong columns; the statistics export gained the same totals row.

## [0.14.0] - 2026-11-18

### Fixed

- The "Refresh" button wiped the table list instead of reloading it: the
  background loader opened the panel's QtSql connection and closed it when it
  finished, so every later query failed silently. Loader and index threads now
  use their own named QtSql connections (`open_connection(conn, tag)`,
  `close_connection(conn, tag)`) and leave the panel connection open.

## [0.13.0] - 2026-11-18

### Changed

- The "Create all spatial indexes" button moved into the table toolbar, right
  after "Export", instead of a separate row above the list.
- The "Information" button was removed from the "Statistics" tab; the dialog is
  still available from the "Tables" tab.

## [0.12.0] - 2026-11-18

### Added

- Status bar connection indicator: a green dot with "Connected" while a
  connection is open and a red dot with "Not connected" otherwise.
- Loading a saved connection from the "Connections" tab connects immediately.

### Changed

- The connect/disconnect button moved from the "Tables" tab to the
  "Connections" tab, next to "Save connection" and "Test connection".
- The saved connection combo selects the first entry when nothing was selected,
  so the "Load" button always has something to load.

## [0.11.0] - 2026-11-18

### Changed

- The "Create all spatial indexes" button moved from the "Statistics" tab to
  the top of the "Tables" tab, next to the other table actions.

## [0.10.0] - 2026-11-18

### Changed

- Tab order in the panel is "Connections", "Tables", "Statistics": the
  connection settings come first.

## [0.9.0] - 2026-11-18

### Changed

- Tab names: the spatial table list is "Tables" again and the connection
  settings tab is "Connections"; the English labels are "Tables" and
  "Connections" respectively.

## [0.8.0] - 2026-11-18

### Added

- Connection parameters are now stored in a local SQLite database
  (`<QGIS profile>/zgeo-pg/connections.sqlite`) in addition to the QGIS
  settings, so host, port, database, user and password survive a restart.
- On startup the plugin imports the saved connections from SQLite into the QGIS
  settings and fills the connection form with the last used connection.
- The name of the last used connection is remembered in both stores.

### Fixed

- Deleting a saved connection removes it from QGIS settings and from SQLite.

## [0.7.0] - 2026-11-18

### Added

- The right-click menu of a table row with an existing spatial index gained
  "Drop spatial index"; it asks for confirmation and removes the index for all
  selected tables.
- `catalog.drop_spatial_index()` and the `drop` action of the background index
  worker.

### Fixed

- The statistics table and its "Spatial index" column are rebuilt after an index
  is created, refreshed or dropped.

## [0.6.0] - 2026-11-18

### Added

- The statistics table gained a "Spatial index" column: "Yes" when every table
  of the schema has an index, "No" otherwise.
- The right-click menu of a table row offers "Create spatial index" when the row
  shows "No" and "Refresh spatial index" when it shows "Yes".
- The statistics table shows a "Create spatial index" menu for rows without an
  index and creates indexes for all tables of that schema.

### Changed

- The bulk button is now called "Create all spatial indexes" and lives at the
  top of the "Statistics" tab.
- Tab order is "Connections", "Connection", "Statistics".

## [0.5.0] - 2026-11-18

### Added

- Right-click menu on the "Statistics" table with "Create spatial index" and
  "Refresh spatial index" for every table of the selected schema.
- "Create spatial index for all layers" button at the top of the "Statistics"
  tab; it builds a GiST index for every loaded table that has none.

### Changed

- Index creation and refresh run in a background thread, report progress in the
  status bar, log per-table errors and refresh the index columns afterwards.

## [0.4.0] - 2026-11-18

### Added

- Table list gained a "Comment" column right after "Table", plus "Spatial index",
  "Spatial index name" and "Spatial index size" columns at the end.
- `catalog.fetch_spatial_index()` reports the GiST/SPGiST index built on the
  geometry column and its size; the background loader fills it in with the
  other characteristics.
- The "Information" dialog shows the table comment and the spatial index
  details.
- The search box also matches table comments.

### Fixed

- The "Information" button is also available on the "Statistics" tab and now
  explains what to do when no single table is selected.
- The Excel export of the table list includes the new columns and keeps the
  totals row aligned with them.

## [0.3.0] - 2026-11-18

### Added

- `change.log` documenting every release in English.
- The build script now leaves only the latest archive in `dist/` and installs
  the plugin into the QGIS Desktop 4.x profile automatically.

### Changed

- README files describe the release workflow and the new `change.log`.

## [0.2.0] - 2026-11-18

### Added

- Complete English translation of the interface: size units, saved connections,
  connection parameters, selections counter, statistics captions, table
  information dialog labels and the plugin action names.
- The panel window title shows the current plugin version.

### Fixed

- Switching the language at runtime now retranslates the connection form labels,
  the saved-connections group, its buttons, the statistics headers and the
  chart titles instead of leaving the previous language in place.
- Thousands separator follows the interface language (space in Russian, comma
  in English).
- The build archive no longer contains Genome service files (`.empryo`).

### Changed

- The first panel tab is now called "Connections" instead of "Tables".

## [0.1.0] - 2026-11-18

### Added

- Initial release: connection to PostgreSQL / PostGIS, list of spatial tables
  with geometry column, geometry type, SRID, estimated rows, exact feature
  count and table size, background loading with cancellation, statistics by
  size, geometry type and schema, and export of lists and tables to Excel.
